### Known verification status — Phase 1 audit, 28 October 2026

- TypeScript: passed (`pnpm exec tsc --noEmit`).
- ESLint: passed (`pnpm lint`).
- Automated tests: 30/30 passed across 4 test files (`pnpm test`).
- Production build: passed (`pnpm build`) after removal of the IMEI test endpoint.
- Routes included in the successful build: `/`, `/api/devices/detect`, `/api/devices/identify`, `/api/devices/image`, `/products`, `/products/[category]`, `/product/[slug]`, and `/_not-found`.
- Upstash connectivity check: HTTP 200, `PONG`.
- IMEI.info connectivity check: HTTP 200 and provider status `Done` for a test-format IMEI. This does not verify the complete Rebox IMEI registration flow.
- Three device-registration RPCs were verified to allow execution for `service_role` but not `anon` or `authenticated`.
- Supabase RLS is enabled on all 18 inspected public tables. Only `products` and `product_variants` had policies in the inspected policy output.
- Broad table-level grants to `anon` and `authenticated` were found on several marketplace tables. Review and least-privilege remediation are outstanding; do not assume grants alone bypass RLS.
- No SQL migration files or `config.toml` were found by the repository searches performed. Migration reproducibility remains unverified.
- The five files `lib/rebox/score.ts`, `lib/rebox/trust.ts`, `lib/rebox/valutaion.ts`, `lib/rebox/diagnostics.ts`, and `lib/rebox/passport.ts` were confirmed to be 0 bytes. A repository search found no direct imports or references to their expected implementations.
- Browser-based device detection currently reports broad device category, operating system, and browser from `navigator.userAgent`; it does not identify an exact hardware model or call the catalogue API.
- The hero animates through device names before settling on the detected category. Review this behavior so it does not imply an incorrect device was detected.
- The hero's “See Your Device Story” link points to `/device`, but no `app/device` page was found in the inspected route inventory. Confirm and fix this destination.
- Image issues observed in development: `/images/branding/rebox-logo.png` returned 404; the Supabase smartwatches category image returned HTTP 400; an image using `fill` lacked a `sizes` prop. These are separate from the IMEI provider connectivity check.
- IMEI provider response parsing and connectivity were inspected, but the original Rebox identification failure has not been traced to a confirmed root cause.
- Component-by-component behavior, all database relationships and constraints, full migration history, duplicate/unused code, and production-readiness remain only partially audited.

## 3. The complete development pathway

The roadmap is organized by dependencies, not merely by website pages. This prevents building features on top of an unreliable device catalogue or unverified backend.

## 01

Audit and stabilize the existing system

Establish what genuinely works before adding more code.

Start here

## 02

Harden the database and backend

Secure and standardize the foundation all features depend on.

## 03

Complete device identification and verification

Establish reliable device identity and evidence.

## 04

Build the Rebox Score and intelligence engine

Convert reliable evidence into transparent assessments.

## 05

Connect the marketplace end to end

Make discovery, listing, buying and selling work together.

## 06

Build accounts, administration and operations

Support real users, transactions, disputes and internal control.

## 07

Quality assurance and launch

Prove the entire product works securely and reliably.

## Phase 1 — Audit and stabilize the existing codebase

Objective: Avoid duplicating work and identify the actual remaining gaps.

### Tasks

- Inspect `package.json`, README, project configuration and environment-variable requirements.
- Map all application pages, reusable components, API routes and service modules.
- Trace each device API from request to database or external provider.
- Run the production build, TypeScript checks, linting and automated tests.
- Inspect existing database migrations, SQL scripts and expected Supabase tables.
- Identify incomplete integrations, placeholder data, hardcoded results and dead code.
- Check that frontend routes and backend endpoints agree.
- Create a status register: completed, partially completed, broken, missing or untested.

### Completion criteria

A code-backed inventory that identifies the state of every major feature, with test results and confirmed defects.

Why first: Without this audit, there is a risk of rebuilding working modules or treating unfinished modules as production-ready.

## Phase 2 — Database and backend foundations

Objective: Create a dependable data layer for the device-intelligence system and marketplace.

### A. Database structure and integrity

- Audit the existing Supabase schema and migrations.
- Verify `devices`, `device_identifiers` and `device_variants`.
- Confirm primary keys, foreign keys, unique constraints and indexes.
- Define how brands, models, model numbers, variants, specifications and identifiers relate.
- Ensure catalogue updates do not create duplicate devices or contradictory variants.
- Define a repeatable process for adding, correcting and reconciling device data.
- Verify development and production database schemas are consistent.

### B. API and security

- Validate every incoming request and reject malformed inputs.
- Keep privileged Supabase credentials on the server.
- Audit Row Level Security (RLS) and permissions.
- Protect sensitive verification data and user records.
- Apply appropriate rate limits to public and expensive verification endpoints.
- Standardize API responses and error handling.
- Add timeouts and safe failure behavior for external providers.
- Ensure logs never expose secrets or unnecessary personal information.

### C. Evidence and device records

- Verify identifier hashing and normalization.
- Verify evidence persistence and physical-device registration.
- Define how evidence is timestamped and linked to the relevant device or verification attempt.
- Prevent duplicate or inconsistent records when requests are retried.
- Decide which fields are authoritative catalogue data and which are user- or provider-supplied.

### Completion criteria

The database has a clear, tested structure; API access is controlled; device records are consistent; and failures do not corrupt stored data.

## Phase 3 — Device intelligence and verification

Objective: Make Rebox reliably identify devices and communicate what is known about them.

This is one of the most important parts of the product because the marketplace's trust claims depend on it.

### A. Real-time device detection

- Verify browser-based device detection works on supported browsers and operating systems.
- Determine what can be identified from browser information and what cannot.
- Match detected identifiers against the Rebox catalogue.
- Handle unsupported devices, unknown models and incomplete browser data.
- Avoid claiming exact identification when the evidence supports only a probable match.
- Ensure the homepage does not rely on hardcoded device information to simulate detection.

### B. Model and variant identification

- Match manufacturer, model, model number and variant.
- Handle regional model numbers and variant differences.
- Support storage, connectivity and other meaningful variant attributes where available.
- Define how confidence is calculated and interpreted.
- Test exact matches, ambiguous matches, missing identifiers and no-match scenarios.
- Improve the catalogue using a controlled ingestion and reconciliation process.

### C. IMEI verification

- Confirm the current IMEI provider and its supported checks.
- Connect provider calls through secure server-side APIs.
- Validate the format and handling of submitted IMEIs.
- Confirm exactly what the provider can return, including blacklist status where supported.
- Distinguish a successful provider response from a failed or unavailable lookup.
- Handle timeouts, rate limits, invalid identifiers and provider outages.
- Avoid storing sensitive identifiers unnecessarily.
- Display the source and time of a verification result where appropriate.

Important distinction: An IMEI lookup is not, by itself, proof that a device is genuine, has never been repaired, is in good physical condition, or is safe to buy. Rebox must distinguish what each check establishes.

### D. Verification reports and device passport

- Connect the device-detection result to the relevant device record.
- Generate a structured report containing supported identity and verification evidence.
- Explain which checks passed, failed, were inconclusive or were not performed.
- Include timestamps and evidence sources where appropriate.
- Verify that passport and report records are consistent with stored evidence.
- Decide which information is public, private or visible only to the owner.

### Completion criteria

A user can identify a supported device, see a clear and appropriately qualified result, and understand the limits of the available evidence.

## Phase 4 — Rebox Score, diagnostics and valuation

Objective: Turn device data into useful intelligence that makes Rebox more than a listing website.

### A. Rebox Score

- Define exactly what the score measures.
- Separate identity confidence, blacklist status, physical condition, ownership evidence and seller/listing trust where applicable.
- Define the inputs, weighting, thresholds and missing-data rules.
- Ensure unsupported checks do not silently count as passing.
- Store enough information to reproduce or explain a historical score.
- Show users the reasons behind the score rather than just a number.
- Test edge cases and prevent inconsistent scores for equivalent evidence.

A single score can be useful, but different dimensions should remain visible so a good condition assessment cannot conceal an unresolved identity or blacklist concern.

### B. Device diagnostics and condition

- Audit the existing diagnostics module.
- Define which checks are possible automatically in a browser and which require user input, special hardware, an app or an in-person inspection.
- Capture diagnostic evidence where technically supported.
- Establish a condition-assessment model for physical damage, screen, battery and other relevant components.
- Distinguish self-reported condition from independently verified condition.
- Record the method, timestamp and limitations of each assessment.

### C. Device valuation and pricing intelligence

- Audit the existing valuation module and correct the `valutaion.ts` filename if appropriate.
- Define valuation inputs such as model, variant, condition, age and market data.
- Establish a reliable source and update schedule for pricing information.
- Account for currency, regional market differences and data freshness.
- Distinguish estimated value from actual market price or a guaranteed resale amount.
- Test the estimate against real listing and transaction data as that data becomes available.

### D. Fraud signals and verification history

- Identify fraud indicators that can be supported by reliable evidence.
- Keep risk signals distinct from proven fraud.
- Record verification history and changes where justified.
- Define how conflicting or outdated evidence affects the score.
- Provide a review process for disputed or corrected results.

### Completion criteria

The Rebox intelligence layer produces traceable assessments, explains their limitations, and can be tested against known cases.

## Phase 5 — Marketplace completion

Objective: Turn the storefront into a functional marketplace that uses Rebox's intelligence.

### A. Catalogue and discovery

- Connect product listings to the authoritative database.
- Complete category and product-detail data flows.
- Implement and verify search.
- Implement filtering and sorting.
- Add pagination or another appropriate catalogue-loading strategy.
- Display product availability and condition accurately.
- Handle empty results, loading states, missing images and server errors.

### B. Product detail and verification

- Display model, variant, specifications, condition and seller information.
- Show verification information relevant to that specific device.
- Distinguish catalogue specifications from inspection results and seller claims.
- Show the Rebox Score and explain its components where supported.
- Provide a clear path to the relevant device report or passport.
- Prevent a generic device verification result from being incorrectly presented as proof about every individual unit of that model.

### C. Seller workflows

- Create seller listing creation and editing.
- Validate product data and listing requirements.
- Associate each listing with the correct device model and variant.
- Capture condition, pricing, availability and relevant evidence.
- Support listing status changes, stock updates and removal.
- Decide whether certain listing types require verification before publication.

### D. Buyer and transaction workflows

- Complete cart functionality if it is part of the chosen purchase model.
- Implement checkout.
- Integrate a suitable payment provider.
- Create order records and track order status.
- Handle successful, failed, pending and cancelled payments.
- Define cancellation, refund, delivery and dispute workflows.
- Ensure payment callbacks and retries cannot create duplicate orders or charges.

### Completion criteria

A buyer can discover a device, understand the listing and its verification status, and complete a supported transaction. A seller can create and manage a listing through the intended workflow.

## Phase 6 — Accounts, administration and operations

Objective: Make Rebox manageable as a real business, not just a public website.

### A. Accounts and profiles

- Implement or verify authentication.
- Build user profiles and account settings.
- Define buyer and seller permissions.
- Associate listings, orders and verification records with the appropriate accounts.
- Apply appropriate access controls to personal and transaction information.

### B. Admin dashboard

- Manage users and account status.
- Review, approve, reject and remove listings.
- Manage the device catalogue and variants.
- Inspect verification reports and failed checks.
- Resolve duplicate or conflicting catalogue records.
- Manage orders, disputes and refunds where applicable.
- Record important administrative actions in audit logs.

### C. Business operations

- Define support and complaint-handling processes.
- Establish seller verification and listing moderation procedures.
- Define dispute resolution and escalation.
- Establish procedures for incorrect verification results and data corrections.
- Create operational reporting for listings, sales, verification activity and failures.

### Completion criteria

The team can manage users, listings, catalogue data, verification cases and transactions through controlled workflows.

## Phase 7 — Quality assurance and launch

Objective: Prove the product is safe, usable and reliable before treating it as production-ready.

### Engineering checks

- Run a clean production build.
- Run TypeScript checks, linting and automated tests.
- Add integration tests for database and provider interactions.
- Test device identification and verification failure cases.
- Test authentication and authorization boundaries.
- Test payment idempotency and transaction states where applicable.
- Verify production environment variables and secrets.
- Review Supabase RLS and storage access policies.

### Product checks

- Test responsive layouts across desktop, tablet and mobile.
- Test keyboard navigation, accessibility and readable error messages.
- Test page performance, image loading and catalogue loading.
- Test the complete buyer journey.
- Test the complete seller journey.
- Test device identification through report generation.
- Test empty states, network failures and provider outages.

### Operations and deployment

- Configure production monitoring and error reporting.
- Establish database backup and recovery procedures.
- Add deployment checks and a rollback process.
- Verify domain, HTTPS, caching and production configuration.
- Define incident handling and support ownership.
- Run a controlled release and fix launch-blocking defects.

### Completion criteria

Critical user journeys pass in a production-like environment, critical security issues are resolved, and deployment and recovery procedures are documented.

## 4. The dependencies that matter most

These are the sequencing rules that should guide implementation:

| Do this first                               | Because it unlocks                                    |
| ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| Audit current code and tests                | Prevents duplicate work and identifies real defects   |
| Stabilize database and APIs                 | Reliable catalogue, verification and marketplace data |
| Complete device identification              | Trust reports, passports and device-specific listings |
| Validate evidence and provider integrations | Meaningful Rebox Score and verification claims        |
| Build the score and valuation rules         | Trust-aware product details and pricing intelligence  |
| Connect marketplace transactions            | Functional buying and selling                         |
| Add admin and operations                    | Safe management of real users and transactions        |
| Complete end-to-end testing                 | A controlled production launch                        |

The catalogue and verification layer should be built as shared infrastructure. Do not create separate, conflicting sources of device specifications for the homepage, product listings and verification reports.

## 5. What should be considered out of scope for the immediate roadmap?

The following should not distract from completing the core product:

- Rebuilding the hero or repeating image-generation work already considered complete.
- Adding more visual sections while critical data flows remain unverified.
- Building elaborate AI features before the catalogue and evidence are reliable.
- Claiming automated hardware diagnostics that browsers or available providers cannot actually perform.
- Displaying a trust score without documented inputs and defensible rules.
- Expanding into every electronics category before the initial device catalogue and verification process are dependable.

A sensible starting scope is mobile phones first, with a data model that can later support laptops, tablets, gaming devices, smartwatches and other electronics.

